The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
k = image->width/ppw;
for (i = image->height; i--; ) {
    ...
    switch (ppw) {
        case 4: /* 8 bpp */
            for (j = k; j; j -= 2, ++src) {
                ...
            }
            break;
        case 2: /* 16 bpp */
            for (j = k; j; j -= 4, ++src) {
                ...
            }
            break;
        case 1: /* 32 bpp */
            for (j = k; j; j -= 8, ++src) {
                ...
            }
            break;
    }
}
```
Reason:
The original implementation calculated `k` based on the image width divided by `ppw`, which assumes that the width is always a multiple of the pixel width. This leads to incorrect processing of trailing pixels when the image width is not aligned to 8-bit boundaries, causing rendering issues.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
for (; j--; ) {
    shift -= ppw;
    *dst++ = colortab[(*src >> shift) & bit_mask];
    if (!shift) {
        shift = 8;
        ++src;
    }
}
```
Reason:
This added loop handles the remaining trailing pixels after the main pixel processing loop. It ensures that any pixels that do not fit into the aligned width are processed correctly, thus fixing the rendering issue for images with arbitrary widths.

